<article class="env-lemma" id="0BHB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BHB">Lemma <span data-tag="0BHB">89.3.1</span>.</a> The functor $F$ (<a href="/tag/0BHA" data-tag="0BHA">89.3.0.1</a>) is an equivalence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      For $n = 1$ this is Limits of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BGY" data-tag="0BGY">70.19.1</a>. For $n > 1$ the lemma can be proved in exactly the same way or it can be deduced from it. For example, suppose that $g_ i : Y_ i \to U_ i$ are objects of $\mathcal{C}_{U_ i, u_ i}$. Then by the case $n = 1$ we can find $f'_ i : Y'_ i \to X$ which are isomorphisms over $X \setminus \{ x_ i\} $ and whose base change to $U_ i$ is $f_ i$. Then we can set 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  f : Y = Y'_1 \times _ X \ldots \times _ X Y'_ n \to X  \]
</div>
    <p>
       This is an object of $\mathcal{C}_{X, \{ x_1, \ldots , x_ n\} }$ whose base change by $U_ i \to X$ recovers $g_ i$. Thus the functor is essentially surjective. We omit the proof of fully faithfulness. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>